  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evaluation is based on a single, highly specific institutional and economic context in London, which may limit its direct application to cities with different planning and taxation powers.",
      "what": "This study evaluates the success of London's Mayoral Community Infrastructure Levy (MCIL), a land value capture mechanism that has raised over £1 billion to help fund the Crossrail transit project. The analysis applies Marsh and McConnell's multi-dimensional policy success framework to assess how the levy balanced revenue generation against the financial viability of private property developments and local borough infrastructure needs.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence on how a major global city successfully implemented a large-scale land value capture program without stalling private real estate development or stripping local municipalities of funding for affordable housing."
    }
